  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# 5.01 — Matrix Lie Groups and Lie Algebras\n\nStage 4 ended by showing that constant curvature and maximal symmetry are the same condition, and it produced the isometry groups of the three model geometries as spaces of **Killing fields**. Those fields form a vector space closed under the Lie bracket. That structure is what Stage 5 is about.\n\n**A Lie group is a group that is also a smooth manifold**, with multiplication and inversion smooth. The ones that matter here are subgroups of $GL_n$ cut out by polynomial equations:\n\n$$SL_n,\\quad O(n),\\ SO(n),\\quad U(n),\\ SU(n),\\quad Sp(2n).$$\n\nEach is a manifold, and Stage 4's machinery applies to it — but the group structure gives something a surface does not have: **the whole manifold looks the same from every point**, because left multiplication $L_g$ is a diffeomorphism carrying the identity anywhere. So all the local geometry is concentrated at one point.\n\n**The Lie algebra is the tangent space at the identity.** Let $A(t)$ be a curve in $G$ with $A(0) = I$, and differentiate the defining equation at $t = 0$. For $O(n)$, from $A^TA = I$,\n\n$$\\dot A^T A + A^T \\dot A = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad X^T + X = 0,$$\n\nso $\\mathfrak{so}(n)$ is the **skew-symmetric** matrices. The same one-line calculation gives every classical algebra:\n\n| group | equation | algebra | condition |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| $SL_n(\\mathbb R)$ | $\\det A = 1$ | $\\mathfrak{sl}_n$ | $\\operatorname{tr}X = 0$ |\n| $O(n),\\ SO(n)$ | $A^TA = I$ | $\\mathfrak{so}(n)$ | $X^T = -X$ |\n| $U(n)$ | $A^\\dagger A = I$ | $\\mathfrak{u}(n)$ | $X^\\dagger = -X$ |\n| $SU(n)$ | $+\\ \\det A = 1$ | $\\mathfrak{su}(n)$ | $X^\\dagger = -X,\\ \\operatorname{tr}X = 0$ |\n| $Sp(2n,\\mathbb R)$ | $A^TJA = J$ | $\\mathfrak{sp}(2n)$ | $X^TJ + JX = 0$ |\n\nA quadratic condition on the group becomes a **linear** condition on the algebra. That is the whole point: the Lie algebra is a vector space, and linear algebra is easy.\n\n**The bracket.** Matrix groups are not commutative, and the failure is measured by\n\n$$[X,Y] = XY - YX.$$\n\nIt is bilinear, antisymmetric, closes on each algebra above, and satisfies the **Jacobi identity**\n\n$$[X,[Y,Z]] + [Y,[Z,X]] + [Z,[X,Y]] = 0.$$\n\nThose three properties are the definition of an abstract Lie algebra. Everything in Stage 5 is built from them.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\n/* A generic real matrix with independent symbolic entries. */\ngenm(sym, n) := genmatrix( lambda([i,j], concat(sym, i, j)), n, n)$\nentries(M) := flatten( args(M) )$\n\n/* The dimension of the solution space of a set of LINEAR conditions:\n   #unknowns − rank of the coefficient matrix.  This is the dimension of\n   the Lie algebra, computed rather than quoted. */\nlin_dim(eqs, vars) := length(vars) - rank( coefmatrix(eqs, vars) )$\n\n/* ---- real algebras ---- */\ndim_gl(n)  := block([X], X : genm(a,n), lin_dim([0], entries(X)))$\ndim_sl(n)  := block([X], X : genm(a,n),\n                lin_dim([ mat_trace(X) ], entries(X)))$\ndim_so(n)  := block([X], X : genm(a,n),\n                lin_dim( entries(X + transpose(X)), entries(X)))$\ndim_sp(m)  := block([X, J, h],           /* m = 2n, must be even */\n                h : m/2,\n                J : matrix_size_helper(h),\n                X : genm(a,m),\n                lin_dim( entries( transpose(X).J + J.X ), entries(X)))$\nmatrix_size_helper(h) := block([Z, I2],\n  Z : zeromatrix(h,h), I2 : ident(h),\n  addcol( addrow(Z, -I2), addrow(I2, Z) ))$\n\n/* ---- complex algebras, as REAL vector spaces: X = A + iB ---- */\n/* X† = Aᵀ − iBᵀ, so X† = −X  ⟺  Aᵀ = −A and Bᵀ = B. */\ndim_u(n)  := block([A, B],\n  A : genm(a,n), B : genm(b,n),\n  lin_dim( append( entries(A + transpose(A)), entries(B - transpose(B)) ),\n           append( entries(A), entries(B) ) ))$\ndim_su(n) := block([A, B],\n  A : genm(a,n), B : genm(b,n),\n  lin_dim( append( entries(A + transpose(A)), entries(B - transpose(B)),\n                   [ mat_trace(A), mat_trace(B) ] ),\n           append( entries(A), entries(B) ) ))$\n\nprint(\"dimensions of the classical algebras, computed as\")$\nprint(\"(number of matrix entries) − (rank of the linear condition):\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   n     so(n)   n(n−1)/2      su(n)   n²−1      sl(n,ℝ)   n²−1\")$\nfor n : 2 thru 5 do\n  print(\"   \", n, \"     \", dim_so(n), \"      \", n*(n-1)/2,\n        \"          \", dim_su(n), \"     \", n^2-1,\n        \"        \", dim_sl(n), \"      \", n^2-1)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   n     u(n)    n²        sp(2n,ℝ)   n(2n+1)\")$\nfor n : 1 thru 4 do\n  print(\"   \", n, \"     \", dim_u(n), \"      \", n^2,\n        \"         \", dim_sp(2*n), \"        \", n*(2*n+1))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Every column agrees with the closed form.  Note so(3) = 3 = su(2):\")$\nprint(\"the two algebras have the same dimension, and 1.03 already showed\")$\nprint(\"the corresponding groups are related by a double cover.\")$\nprint(\"Note also sp(2,ℝ) = 3 = sl(2,ℝ) — in fact they are equal as sets,\")$\nprint(\"since the condition XᵀJ + JX = 0 in 2×2 reduces to tr X = 0.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 1,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "dimensions of the classical algebras, computed as\n(number of matrix entries) − (rank of the linear condition):\n   n     so(n)   n(n−1)/2      su(n)   n²−1      sl(n,ℝ)   n²−1\n    2       1        1            3       3          3        3\n    3       3        3            8       8          8        8\n    4       6        6            15       15          15        15\n    5       10        10            24       24          24        24\n   n     u(n)    n²        sp(2n,ℝ)   n(2n+1)\n    1       1        1           3          3\n    2       4        4           10          10\n    3       9        9           21          21\n    4       16        16           36          36\nEvery column agrees with the closed form.  Note so(3) = 3 = su(2):\nthe two algebras have the same dimension, and 1.03 already showed\nthe corresponding groups are related by a double cover.\nNote also sp(2,ℝ) = 3 = sl(2,ℝ) — in fact they are equal as sets,\nsince the condition XᵀJ + JX = 0 in 2×2 reduces to tr X = 0."
          ]
        }
