    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "/* --- su(2): the same structure constants, a different group --- */\nsig : [ matrix([0,1],[1,0]), matrix([0,-%i],[%i,0]), matrix([1,0],[0,-1]) ]$\nEb  : makelist( ratsimp(-%i/2 * sig[k]), k, 1, 3)$\n\nprint(\"su(2), basis Eₖ = −(i/2)σₖ:\")$\nfor k:1 thru 3 do\n  print(\"   E\", k, \" = \", Eb[k],\n        \"    anti-Hermitian & traceless ? \",\n        is( ratsimp(Eb[k] + conjugate(transpose(Eb[k]))) = zeromatrix(2,2)\n            and ratsimp(mat_trace(Eb[k])) = 0 ))$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"structure constants, against the same ε_ijk:\")$\nfor i:1 thru 3 do for j:1 thru 3 do\n  if i < j then block([k], k : 6 - i - j,\n    print(\"   [E\", i, \", E\", j, \"] − ε_\", i, j, k, \" E\", k, \" = \",\n          ratsimp( br(Eb[i], Eb[j]) - eps(i,j,k)*Eb[k] )) )$\nprint(\"   Identical to so(3).  su(2) ≅ so(3) as Lie algebras — the\")$\nprint(\"   isomorphism is Eₖ ↦ Lₖ, and nothing else needs checking, because\")$\nprint(\"   a Lie algebra IS its structure constants.\")$\n\n/* --- the quaternion basis (1.03) is the same thing again --- */\nqi : ratsimp(2*Eb[1])$  qj : ratsimp(2*Eb[2])$  qk : ratsimp(2*Eb[3])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"with qᵢ = 2Eᵢ (the quaternion units of 1.03):\")$\nprint(\"   qi² = \", ratsimp(qi.qi), \"   qj² = \", ratsimp(qj.qj),\n      \"   qk² = \", ratsimp(qk.qk))$\nprint(\"   qi qj = \", ratsimp(qi.qj), \"   = qk ? \", is(ratsimp(qi.qj) = qk))$\nprint(\"   qj qi = \", ratsimp(qj.qi), \"   = −qk ? \", is(ratsimp(qj.qi) = -qk))$\nprint(\"   qi qj qk = \", ratsimp(qi.qj.qk),\n      \"  — Hamilton's i² = j² = k² = ijk = −1, as 2×2 complex matrices.\")$\n\n/* --- the double cover, made visible by a period --- */\n/* matrixexp returns complex exponentials; demoivre then trigrat puts the\n   rotation back into cos/sin form.  (rectform alone makes it worse.) */\ntidy(M) := matrixmap( lambda([e], trigrat(demoivre(e))), M )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"SO(3) and SU(2) are NOT the same group.  Exponentiate the third\")$\nprint(\"generator in each and compare periods:\")$\nRz : tidy( matrixexp(Lb[3], th) )$\nUz : ratsimp( matrixexp(Eb[3], th) )$\nprint(\"   exp(θ L₃) = \", Rz)$\nprint(\"      — rotation by θ about the z-axis, exactly as in 1.03\")$\nprint(\"   exp(θ E₃) = \", Uz)$\nprint(\"\")$\nfor t in [0, 2*%pi, 4*%pi] do\n  print(\"   θ = \", t, \" :   exp(θL₃) = \", tidy(subst(th=t, Rz)),\n        \"   exp(θE₃) = \", ratsimp(demoivre(subst(th=t, Uz))))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"exp(θL₃) closes up at θ = 2π; exp(θE₃) returns to −I there and needs\")$\nprint(\"4π to come home.  That factor of two IS the double cover SU(2) → SO(3):\")$\nprint(\"same Lie algebra, same local geometry, different global topology\")$\nprint(\"(S³ versus ℝP³).  The Lie algebra cannot see it.\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "su(2), basis Eₖ = −(i/2)σₖ:\n   E 1  =  matrix([0,-(%i/2)],[-(%i/2),0])     anti-Hermitian & traceless ?\n    true\n   E 2  =  matrix([0,-(1/2)],[1/2,0])     anti-Hermitian & traceless ?  true\n   E 3  =  matrix([-(%i/2),0],[0,%i/2])     anti-Hermitian & traceless ?  true\nstructure constants, against the same ε_ijk:\n   [E 1 , E 2 ] − ε_ 1 2 3  E 3  =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])\n   [E 1 , E 3 ] − ε_ 1 3 2  E 2  =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])\n   [E 2 , E 3 ] − ε_ 2 3 1  E 1  =  matrix([0,0],[0,0])\n   Identical to so(3).  su(2) ≅ so(3) as Lie algebras — the\n   isomorphism is Eₖ ↦ Lₖ, and nothing else needs checking, because\n   a Lie algebra IS its structure constants.\nwith qᵢ = 2Eᵢ (the quaternion units of 1.03):\n   qi² =  matrix([-1,0],[0,-1])    qj² =  matrix([-1,0],[0,-1])    qk² =\n         matrix([-1,0],[0,-1])\n   qi qj =  matrix([-%i,0],[0,%i])    = qk ?  true\n   qj qi =  matrix([%i,0],[0,-%i])    = −qk ?  true\n   qi qj qk =  matrix([-1,0],[0,-1])\n                — Hamilton's i² = j² = k² = ijk = −1, as 2×2 complex matrices.\nSO(3) and SU(2) are NOT the same group.  Exponentiate the third\ngenerator in each and compare periods:\n   exp(θ L₃) =  matrix([cos(th),-sin(th),0],[sin(th),cos(th),0],[0,0,1])\n      — rotation by θ about the z-axis, exactly as in 1.03\n   exp(θ E₃) =  matrix([%e^-((%i*th)/2),0],[0,%e^((%i*th)/2)])\n   θ =  0  :   exp(θL₃) =  matrix([1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1])    exp(θE₃) =\n       matrix([1,0],[0,1])\n   θ =  2*%pi  :   exp(θL₃) =  matrix([1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1])    exp(θE₃) =\n       matrix([-1,0],[0,-1])\n   θ =  4*%pi  :   exp(θL₃) =  matrix([1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1])    exp(θE₃) =\n       matrix([1,0],[0,1])\nexp(θL₃) closes up at θ = 2π; exp(θE₃) returns to −I there and needs\n4π to come home.  That factor of two IS the double cover SU(2) → SO(3):\nsame Lie algebra, same local geometry, different global topology\n(S³ versus ℝP³).  The Lie algebra cannot see it."
