      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n$$[X,Y] = XY - YX, \\qquad \\mathfrak g = T_IG$$\n\n**Dimensions, computed not quoted.** Each classical algebra was found as the null space of its defining *linear* condition — number of matrix entries minus the rank of the constraint — and every entry matched the closed form for $n$ up to 5:\n\n| algebra | condition | dimension |\n|---|---|---|\n| $\\mathfrak{so}(n)$ | $X^T = -X$ | $n(n-1)/2$ |\n| $\\mathfrak{su}(n)$ | $X^\\dagger = -X$, $\\operatorname{tr}X=0$ | $n^2-1$ |\n| $\\mathfrak u(n)$ | $X^\\dagger = -X$ | $n^2$ |\n| $\\mathfrak{sp}(2n,\\mathbb R)$ | $X^TJ + JX = 0$ | $n(2n+1)$ |\n\nTwo coincidences fall out of the table: $\\dim\\mathfrak{so}(3) = \\dim\\mathfrak{su}(2) = 3$, and $\\mathfrak{sp}(2,\\mathbb R) = \\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb R)$ — in the $2\\times2$ case the symplectic condition collapses to $\\operatorname{tr}X = 0$.\n\n**The cross product is a Lie bracket.** The hat map $v \\mapsto \\hat v$ sending $\\mathbb{R}^3$ to $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ satisfies $[\\hat v,\\hat w] = \\widehat{v \\times w}$ identically, and $\\hat v w = v \\times w$. So the vector algebra of 1.01 was $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ all along, and the Jacobi identity for the bracket is the familiar $a\\times(b\\times c) + b\\times(c\\times a) + c\\times(a\\times b) = 0$. The structure constants are $\\varepsilon_{ijk}$ — including $[L_1,L_3] = -L_2$, which is the sign $\\varepsilon_{132} = -1$ and not a slip.\n\n**Same algebra, different group.** $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ has *identical* structure constants to $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$, so the algebras are isomorphic and there is nothing further to check — a Lie algebra is its structure constants. But $\\exp(\\theta L_3)$ returns to $I$ at $\\theta = 2\\pi$ while $\\exp(\\theta E_3)$ reaches $-I$ there and needs $4\\pi$. That factor of two is the double cover $SU(2) \\to SO(3)$ of 1.03, now visible as a period. Setting $q_k = 2E_k$ recovers Hamilton's relations $i^2 = j^2 = k^2 = ijk = -1$ exactly, as $2\\times2$ complex matrices.\n\n**The Lie algebra sees local structure only.** $S^3$ and $\\mathbb{RP}^3$ have the same $\\mathfrak g$; the difference is entirely global.\n\n**4.07's Killing fields are $\\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb R)$.** The Möbius action of $M \\in \\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb R)$ on the upper half-plane induces the field $F(z) = b + (a-d)z - cz^2$, which reproduces exactly the three Killing fields found in 4.07 (up to scale and sign), each verified Killing here again.\n\nThe bracket, though, comes out **negated**: $\\mathrm{ind}([A,B]) = -[\\mathrm{ind}A, \\mathrm{ind}B]$ in all three cases, with the sums identically zero. This is the standard anti-homomorphism of an infinitesimal left action, not an error — the homomorphism convention flows along $\\exp(-tX)$ instead, and texts differ on which they take. The image is $\\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb R)$ either way.\n\n**The Erlangen groups, as algebras.**\n\n| Stage 2 group | algebra | curvature |\n|---|---|---|\n| $E(2)$ | $\\mathfrak{so}(2) \\ltimes \\mathbb{R}^2$, not simple | $K = 0$ |\n| $SO(3)$ | $\\mathfrak{so}(3) \\cong \\mathfrak{su}(2)$, compact | $K > 0$ |\n| $PSL_2(\\mathbb R)$ | $\\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb R)$, non-compact | $K < 0$ |\n\nCompactness of the group tracks the sign of the curvature — which 5.03 makes precise via the Killing form.\n\n## Next\n\n**5.02 — The Exponential Map and Adjoint** studies $\\exp: \\mathfrak g \\to G$ properly: one-parameter subgroups, where $\\exp$ fails to be surjective or injective, the adjoint representations $\\mathrm{Ad}$ and $\\mathrm{ad}$, and the Baker–Campbell–Hausdorff formula that measures how badly $\\exp(X)\\exp(Y)$ differs from $\\exp(X+Y)$.\n\n## References\n\n- Hall, B.C. (2015) *Lie Groups, Lie Algebras, and Representations: An Elementary Introduction*. 2nd edn. Graduate Texts in Mathematics 222. Cham: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-3-319-13467-3](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-13467-3). The matrix-groups-first treatment this notebook follows; Chapters 1–3.\n- ★ Hall, B.C. (2013) *Quantum Theory for Mathematicians*. New York: Springer. Chapter 16 covers the same ground — matrix Lie groups, Lie algebras, the matrix exponential — more briefly, and is the copy actually to hand here.\n- Stillwell, J. (2008) *Naive Lie Theory*. Undergraduate Texts in Mathematics. New York: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-0-387-78214-0](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-78214-0). The most accessible entry point and the closest to this journey's level; it reaches $SU(2) \\to SO(3)$ through quaternions, in the same spirit as 1.03.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2013) *Introduction to Smooth Manifolds*. 2nd edn. Graduate Texts in Mathematics 218. New York: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-1-4419-9982-5](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-9982-5). Chapters 7, 8 and 20 for Lie groups as manifolds; Chapter 9 for the sign in the infinitesimal-action anti-homomorphism found above.\n- Fulton, W. and Harris, J. (1991) *Representation Theory: A First Course*. Graduate Texts in Mathematics 129. New York: Springer. Where the classification of simple Lie algebras lives, if that is the direction of travel.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = in the local indexed library (`maths` collection); the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell was executed. The bracket sign in the last cell is printed output, not a claim: the sums are identically zero, which is what an anti-homomorphism looks like.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}